Identity and Access Management (IAM) :-

To create an account we need 3 things:- Uniq mail Id, Credit card 

Once the account is created it also create a root user.  Root user has access to everything in the account. 
It can perform any action on any resources within the account. 

IAM is the service that is responsible for identity and access management allow users to connect and be able to perform a verity of diff operation within aws. 

It manages who has access to what in your aws account. They handle the authetication and authorisation process.
Types of Identities:- User, Groups, Roles.

IAM Policies:- It defines what resources a user or a group or a role can have access to end what actions they can perform on them. 

user:-  When a new user is created by default user dont have access to any of the resources within aws.
	Iam policies is just a document that either grants or denies acccess to a specific service or resource.
	Always follows the principle of least previllages.

Groups:- Groups are nothing more than just a collection if IAM users that ideally are going to have similar IAM Policies. 
	 You can assign policies to the group. whan you take a user and assign them to the group they inherit the policies.
	 It is little bit more efficient than assigning an individual policy for every single user.

Role:- Role allow user to assume or borrow permissions of that role for short period of time. 
       Used when you need to grant access to a service on a temporary besis. 


Multi Factor Authentication:-  It is for te extra security for your account. You can make use of it and you can have a code sent to you MFA device or app. you have to provide that code to aws to login.
 You have to enable it per user besis manually.
 

